dialogue is: {{ Doctor: Good morning, Carolyn. How are you today?
Patient: Good morning, doctor. I'm okay, thank you.
Doctor: I see you're here for a follow-up of your chronic problem. Can you tell me more about it?
Patient: Yes, my osteoarthritis has been flaring up a little lately, and I would like to talk about that, primarily in my elbows.
Doctor: Hmm, I see. Can you tell me more about your elbow pain?
Patient: My right elbow bothers me more than my left elbow, and I am primarily typing all day versus writing.
Doctor: I understand. Do you have any other joint pain?
Patient: No, I don't have any other joint pain.
Doctor: How about numbness and tingling in your hands?
Patient: Yes, I have occasional numbness and tingling in my hands, but I try to shake it out by shaking my arms.
Doctor: Okay, I see. Do you have a history of gout?
Patient: Yes, I do.
Doctor: When was your last episode of gout?
Patient: It was about 3 months ago.
Doctor: And what happened during that time?
Patient: I had some inflammation of my toe at that time.
Doctor: I see. Did you have any other issues with that?
Patient: No, I didn't have any other issues.
Doctor: And did the medication help with your gout?
Patient: Yes, the medication helped, and I haven't seen a flare up since.
Doctor: That's good to hear. How about your psoriasis?
Patient: My psoriasis is under control and has not been a major problem for me.
Doctor: I see. Are you still taking colchicine for your gout?
Patient: Yes, I am still on colchicine 0.6 mg twice a day as needed for flare-ups of my gout.
Doctor: Okay, I see. Let's do a physical examination now. Can you tell me about your neck?
Patient: My neck is supple without lymphadenopathy.
Doctor: Okay. How about your respiratory system?
Patient: My lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.
Doctor: Good. How about your cardiovascular system?
Patient: My heart rate and rhythm are regular.
Doctor: And your gastrointestinal system?
Patient: My abdomen is soft.
Doctor: Okay. How about your musculoskeletal system?
Patient: There is no edema to the lower extremity. Examination of the right elbow, there is some edema and inflammation of the right olecranon. Some tenderness to palpation of the olecranon bursa. Pain with pronation and supination. Pain with flexion and extension of the right arm.
Doctor: I see. Based on your history and physical examination, I have diagnosed you with gout and osteoarthritis. We will continue your colchicine for gout and prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once a day for pain and swelling for your osteoarthritis. We will also continue your steroid cream for your psoriasis. Do you have any questions or concerns about the treatment plan?
Patient: No, I don't have any questions or concerns. Thank you, doctor.
Doctor: You're welcome. Please come back for a follow-up in two weeks.
Patient: Okay, I will. Thank you, doctor.

