dialogue is: {{ 
Doctor: Hi, I'm Dr. Smith. What brings you here today?

Patient: Hi, I have joint pain in my left elbow.

Doctor: I see. Can you tell me more about your joint pain?

Patient: Yes, it's been bothering me for a while now. I sit at my desk all day for work with my elbows bent and it's really hard for me to flex and extend my left elbow after work. It's really stiff and painful.

Doctor: Hmm, I understand. Have you noticed any swelling on your left elbow?

Patient: Yes, I have noticed swelling on the olecranon of my elbow.

Doctor: Okay. Are you right-handed?

Patient: Yes, I am.

Doctor: That's good. Can you still write with your right hand?

Patient: Yes, I can still write, which is great, but typing is difficult.

Doctor: I see. Have you experienced any numbness or tingling in your hands?

Patient: No, I haven't.

Doctor: Okay. How about weakness in your arm?

Patient: I have some weakness in my arm, but nothing too significant.

Doctor: I see. Have you had any fever or chills?

Patient: No, I haven't.

Doctor: Okay. Can you tell me about your past medical history?

Patient: I have epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease.

Doctor: I see. Have you had any recent seizures?

Patient: No, I haven't had any recent seizures. My last seizure was a year ago.

Doctor: Okay. Are you taking any medication for your epilepsy?

Patient: Yes, I'm still taking Keppra.

Doctor: I see. And how about your depression?

Patient: I started therapy last year and I'm trying to avoid medications for this problem. Therapy has been helpful, but I still feel down and stressed.

Doctor: I see. Have you had any suicidal or homicidal ideations?

Patient: No, I haven't.

Doctor: Okay. Can you tell me about your peptic ulcer?

Patient: I was dealing with a lot of stress last year and underwent an endoscopy which showed that I had an episode of gastritis. I was subsequently diagnosed with peptic ulcer disease and started Protonix. I haven't had any issues with my ulcer since the procedure and everything has been good.

Doctor: I see. Have you had any chest pain, shortness of breath, abdominal pain, nausea, or vomiting?

Patient: No, I haven't had any of those symptoms.

Doctor: Okay. How about medial nasal congestion from allergies?

Patient: Yes, I have a little bit of medial nasal congestion from allergies.

Doctor: Okay. I'm going to examine your left elbow now. Can you tell me if you feel any pain when I touch it?

Patient: Yes, it hurts when you touch it.

Doctor: Okay. The examination of your left elbow reveals erythema and edema. There is pain to palpation of the left olecranon. It's warm to palpation and there is pain with flexion and extension. The palpable left radial pulse is present.

Doctor: The x-ray of your left arm does not reveal any evidence of elbow fracture. The EGD revealed an episode of gastritis.

Doctor: Based on your symptoms and examination, I believe you have left olecranon bursitis caused by repetitive trauma. I'm going to start you on meloxicam 15 mg once daily. I encourage you to use a pillow to rest your arm on and ice the area.

Doctor: I'm also going to continue your Keppra 500 mg twice a day and start you on Lexapro 10 mg once daily for your depression.

Doctor: And for your peptic ulcer, I'm going to continue Protonix 40 mg once daily before meals.

Doctor: Do you understand and agree with the recommended medical treatment plan?

Patient: Yes, I understand and agree with the recommended medical treatment plan.

Doctor: Great. If you have any questions or concerns, please don't hesitate to ask.

