Doctor: Good morning, Ms. Thompson. How are you feeling today?

Patient: I'm feeling okay, doctor.

Doctor: Can you tell me what brings you in today?

Patient: Yes, I have joint pain.

Doctor: I see. Can you tell me more about your joint pain?

Patient: I've been swimming more regularly and trying to be more active, but my right knee has been hurting. It starts hurting after a couple of laps.

Doctor: I understand. Have you had any injury or trauma to your knee?

Patient: No, I haven't had any injury or trauma.

Doctor: Okay. And how has your chronic back pain been?

Patient: It's been intermittent, but it's been doing well lately.

Doctor: That's good to hear. And how has your depression been?

Patient: It's been going really well with my medication and therapy. I've been trying to get outside more and it's been helping with my depression. I'm sleeping and eating well and I don't have any concerns about hurting myself or others.

Doctor: That's great to hear. And how has your carpal tunnel been?

Patient: It's been doing really well. I've been getting full range of motion and it's felt a lot better.

Doctor: That's good to hear. Let's take a look at your physical examination. Your neurological and psychological exam shows appropriate mood and affect. Your grips are equal bilaterally. Your neck is supple without thyromegaly or cervical lymphadenopathy. Your lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally and there are no wheezes, rales, or rhonchi. Your cardiovascular exam shows a regular rate and rhythm with no murmurs, gallops, or rubs and no extra heart sounds. Your musculoskeletal exam shows no lower extremity edema, but there is pain with patellar inhibition and pain with range of motion of the right knee.

Doctor: We also did an x-ray of your right knee and it was unremarkable.

Doctor: Based on your history and examination, we have diagnosed you with Chondromalacia of the right patella. This is a condition where the cartilage under the patella is soft and worn away. It's not uncommon in active individuals like yourself. We have prescribed ibuprofen 600 mg 4 times a day with food to help with the pain. We also want you to do some straight leg raising exercises to strengthen your quadriceps. It's important to participate in physical activity that is less straining on your joints, such as swimming freestyle laps, or to stop activity altogether. If your pain does not improve in a couple of weeks, we will need to follow up.

Doctor: Your chronic back pain is stable and well-controlled at this time, so we don't need to make any changes to your current regimen. And your depression is also doing well with your current medications, so we will continue with your current regimen.

Doctor: And your carpal tunnel syndrome is relatively stable status post carpal tunnel release, so we won't be making any changes to your current regimen at this time.

Doctor: Do you have any questions or concerns about the treatment plan?

Patient: No, I understand and agree with the recommended medical treatment plan.

Doctor: Great. If you have any questions or concerns in the future, don't hesitate to reach out to us.

