Doctor: Hi, how are you feeling today?

Patient: I'm feeling tired and lacking a lot of energy.

Doctor: I see. Can you tell me more about your symptoms?

Patient: Yes, I have swollen elbow joints, body aches, bullseye rash, and nausea. I haven't been able to eat much, just crackers.

Doctor: Hmm, okay. And have you had any high fevers?

Patient: No, I haven't had any high fevers.

Doctor: Alright. Can you tell me about your past medical history?

Patient: I have type 2 diabetes and hypertension.

Doctor: I see. And how have your blood sugars been lately?

Patient: They've been okay, sometimes a little high or low, but not too far out of range.

Doctor: Okay, and what medication are you taking for your diabetes?

Patient: I'm still taking metformin.

Doctor: And how about your hypertension, have you been monitoring your blood pressure at home?

Patient: Yes, it's been good. I've been avoiding salty foods and taking lisinopril.

Doctor: That's good to hear. Have you had any abdominal pain or diarrhea?

Patient: No, I haven't had any abdominal pain or diarrhea.

Doctor: Alright. I'm going to examine you now. Can you tell me if you have any swelling or pain in your elbow joints?

Patient: Yes, I have swelling and pain in both of my elbow joints.

Doctor: And have you noticed any rashes on your skin?

Patient: Yes, I have a small bullseye rash on my left abdominal quadrant.

Doctor: Alright. I'm going to order some tests for you. Your Lyme titer came back elevated, which means you have Lyme disease.

Patient: Oh, okay.

Doctor: I'm going to prescribe doxycycline 100 mg twice a day for 3 weeks. I'll also touch base with an infectious disease doctor to make sure we're covering all our bases. I want to see you again next week for a follow-up.

Patient: Alright, sounds good.

Doctor: And I'm going to order a lipid panel for your hypertension. You'll continue taking lisinopril 10 mg a day, which has been refilled today. Just keep monitoring your blood pressure at home and limit your sodium intake.

Patient: Okay, I understand.

Doctor: And for your diabetes, I'm going to order a hemoglobin A1c to make sure we don't need to make any changes to your medication. Just keep taking metformin and try to eat as much as you can, even if it's just crackers.

Patient: Alright, I will.

Doctor: Great. And just to make sure, do you understand and agree with the recommended medical treatment plan?

Patient: Yes, I understand and agree.

Doctor: Alright, great. If you have any questions or concerns, don't hesitate to ask.

Patient: Okay, thank you.

