Doctor: Hi Emily, it's great to see you again. How are you feeling today?

Emily: I'm feeling good, thank you.

Doctor: That's great to hear. So, you're here for a follow-up for your asthma, allergic rhinitis, and food allergy, correct?

Emily: Yes, that's right.

Doctor: Can you tell me a little bit about your history of present illness?

Emily: Sure. I have asthma and allergic rhinitis, and I'm also allergic to peanuts and tree nuts.

Doctor: I see. And have you had any recent symptoms or issues with your asthma or allergies?

Emily: No, I haven't had any issues recently.

Doctor: That's good to hear. And I see that you've been switched from Symbicort to Wixela. How has that been working for you?

Emily: It's been working well. I've been reducing my daily dose to one puff per day, 3-4 days per week.

Doctor: I see. And you're also trying to join the military, correct?

Emily: Yes, I am. I need to meet certain physical fitness requirements without the use of my inhaler.

Doctor: I understand. And you've been using Zyrtec for your allergic rhinitis, correct?

Emily: Yes, I use it primarily during the spring and fall, and sometimes in the summer. I take it 3 times per week when I work outside.

Doctor: Okay, and have you had any issues with your food allergies recently?

Emily: No, I've been avoiding peanuts and tree nuts successfully.

Doctor: Great. And I see that you've been using Alvesco 80 mcg 2 puff 2 times daily. How has that been working for you?

Emily: It's been working well.

Doctor: Okay, good. And I see that you've also been using an EpiPen. Have you had to use it recently?

Emily: No, I haven't had to use it recently.

Doctor: Okay, good. And I see that you've been smoking and vaping, but you're trying to quit. How's that going?

Emily: I've been trying to reduce my use, and I think I've made some progress.

Doctor: That's great to hear. And I see that you have a nebulizer, but you haven't needed it recently. Is that correct?

Emily: Yes, that's correct. I only use it for upper respiratory infections or when I was a child a few times.

Doctor: Okay, good. And I see that your Asthma Control Test score is 21. That's great. And your spirometry test today was better than it was in 2019.

Emily: Yes, I recall that it was smoky from wildfires during my last breathing test.

Doctor: I see. Well, it looks like you're doing well. I'll refill your EpiPen and I'll give you some instructions for your follow-up.

Doctor: For your moderate persistent asthma, I want you to start using Alvesco 2 puffs twice daily. Keep your Wixela in case Alvesco doesn't work well. Continue using your albuterol as needed. And come back in 4 months to repeat your ACT and spirometry testing.

Emily: Okay, I understand.

Doctor: And for your allergic rhinitis, continue taking Zyrtec 10 mg daily as needed. And for your anaphylactic reaction due to peanuts, continue your strict avoidance of peanuts.

Emily: Okay, I will.

Doctor: Great. If you have any questions or concerns, don't hesitate to ask.

Emily: Okay, thank you.

