Doctor: Good morning, Mr. Allen. How are you feeling today?

Patient: Good morning, doctor. I'm feeling okay.

Doctor: I understand that you came in today for an evaluation of a right shoulder injury. Can you tell me more about it?

Patient: Yes, I was playing football a few days ago and I was tackled a couple of times and landed on my right shoulder. I had pain since then.

Doctor: Hmm, I see. Where exactly do you feel the pain?

Patient: The pain is located on the back of my shoulder.

Doctor: Do you have any numbness or tingling in your fingers?

Patient: No, I don't have any numbness or tingling.

Doctor: Okay. And how is the strength in your hand?

Patient: The strength is okay, but I have been trying to avoid lifting anything too heavy.

Doctor: I see. Can you touch your shoulder to your ear?

Patient: No, I can't because of the pain.

Doctor: Okay. Have you been taking any medication for the pain?

Patient: Yes, I've been taking Tylenol, but it doesn't provide much relief.

Doctor: I see. Do you have any neck problems or other joint problems?

Patient: No, I don't have any neck problems or other joint problems.

Doctor: Have you had any previous orthopedic injuries to your right shoulder?

Patient: No, I haven't had any previous orthopedic injuries to my right shoulder.

Doctor: Have you had any fever or chills since the injury?

Patient: No, I haven't had any fever or chills, but I do have a headache.

Doctor: I see. Can you tell me more about the headache?

Patient: I hit my head at the time of the injury, but I didn't pass out.

Doctor: I see. Can you tell me about your surgical history?

Patient: I had an appendectomy 3 to 4 years ago.

Doctor: Okay. I see. Let's take a look at your vital signs. Your blood pressure is 122/76, heart rate is 82 beats per minute, and your oxygen saturation is 95% on room air.

Doctor: During the physical examination, I found that there is pain to palpation of the posterior shoulder at the acromioclavicular joint. There is decreased abduction and adduction to about 90 degrees only. Pain with supination and pronation of the right arm. Palpable radial artery pulse.

Doctor: The x-ray of your right shoulder revealed an acute acromioclavicular joint fracture.

Doctor: Based on the examination and radiographic findings, I have had a lengthy discussion with you about your current symptoms. Your x-rays showed an acute acromioclavicular joint fracture. I have prescribed meloxicam 15 mg once a day to treat your pain and I recommend that we obtain a CT scan of the right shoulder to evaluate further. I also recommend that you attend formal physical therapy to strengthen your right shoulder in hopes of avoiding surgery in the future. In the meantime, I advise you to avoid tackle football.

Patient: Okay, doctor. Thank you for your help.

Doctor: You're welcome, Mr. Allen. If you have any questions or concerns, please don't hesitate to ask.

