Doctor: Hello, how are you feeling today?

Patient: I'm feeling okay, but I have been having shortness of breath and coughing up blood.

Doctor: I see. Can you tell me more about your medical history?

Patient: Yes, I have diabetes, hypertension, and asthma. And I have a long history of knee pain.

Doctor: Okay, I see. And what medications are you taking for these conditions?

Patient: I am taking metformin and Norvasc.

Doctor: Alright. Can you tell me if you have any other symptoms like fever, bleeding gums, or bruising?

Patient: No, I don't have a fever. And I don't have any bleeding gums. But I have been coughing up blood and having shortness of breath.

Doctor: I see. Can you tell me more about your knee pain?

Patient: It's been bothering me for a long time.

Doctor: Alright. Let's take a look at your vital signs. Your pulse oxygenation is 94 percent and your blood pressure is 120/70 mm Hg.

Doctor: During the physical exam, I noticed some rales and rhonchi in your lungs, more on the right side. And there is some diminished breath sounds in the right as well. But there is no wheezing right now.

Doctor: I also listened to your heart and it has a regular rate and a 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, which is unchanged from before.

Doctor: I examined your abdomen and it is soft. And I found 1+ nonpitting edema on your bilateral lower extremities, but no calf tenderness and negative Homan's sign.

Doctor: Based on your symptoms and examination, I think it's important for you to get further testing done. Your shortness of breath could be as simple as a pneumonia or something more serious.

Doctor: I have ordered a chest x-ray, CT scan, and blood work to be done in the ER. And I would like you to go to the ER immediately after this appointment. I will also call ahead.

Doctor: I have also noticed that your blood sugar has not been well controlled, so I have ordered an updated A1c today. And I would like you to increase your metformin to 750 mg twice daily.

Doctor: And I think your blood pressure has been running low for about 3 weeks, which could be caused by your shortness of breath. So, I would like to get a better understanding of the cause of your shortness of breath prior to pursuing further treatment for your hypertension.

Doctor: At this time, I would like you to discontinue the use of Norvasc.

Patient: Okay, I understand and agree with the recommended medical treatment plan.

Doctor: Great. We will have you follow up in about 3 weeks to discuss your diabetes and further treatment recommendations.

