dialogue is: {{ Doctor: Hello Scott, how are you feeling today?
Patient: I'm doing okay, thanks for asking.
Doctor: I see from your chief complaint that you have a right knee injury. Can you tell me more about that?
Patient: Yeah, I was playing pickup soccer games a few days ago and felt a pop in my right knee. I had swelling at the time and it's improved, but my knee isn't moving as well as it used to.
Doctor: I see. Have you been taking anything for the pain?
Patient: Yes, I've been taking ibuprofen regularly and it helps a bit.
Doctor: Okay, and on a scale of 1 to 10, how would you rate your pain level?
Patient: It's around a 6 to 7 right now, but it's around a 4 to 5 when the swelling decreases.
Doctor: I see. Have you been able to bear weight on your right knee?
Patient: Yes, but it feels loose and doesn't feel normal.
Doctor: Alright, I'm going to examine your knee now. I see that you have a marked limp in your right leg upon ambulation. Upon examination, there is significant effusion and slight ecchymosis. I also feel pain with palpation at the medial joint line and there is difficulty with passive ROM. You lack a few degrees of terminal extension to 115 degrees and lack 20-30 degrees of terminal flexion due to pain. I also have a positive Lachman's test. Your muscle strength is 5/5 and neurovascularly you are intact distally.
Patient: Okay, what does that mean?
Doctor: Based on your examination findings, it is possible that you have an anterior cruciate ligament tear. I recommend that we obtain an MRI of your right knee to evaluate the integrity of the ACL. In the meantime, I've prescribed meloxicam 15 mg a day to reduce swelling and pain. I also recommend using ice and activity modification to reduce swelling and pain. You should wear a knee immobilizer and use crutches when ambulating, and avoid jogging until we have the results of your MRI.
Patient: Okay, thank you for explaining that to me.
Doctor: Of course, we will follow up once the MRI results are available for review and further discussion. }}<|eot_id|>
