dialogue is: {{ Doctor: Good morning, how are you feeling today?
Patient: Good morning, I'm feeling okay, but I have some pain in my right elbow.
Doctor: Can you tell me more about your pain? When did it start?
Patient: It started 4 days ago and it's still there. I can't use my pottery wheel because of it.
Doctor: I see. Have you had any injury to your right elbow recently?
Patient: No, I haven't had any injury, but I've been using my pottery wheel more often.
Doctor: Hmm, I understand. Can you describe the pain for me?
Patient: The pain is in the inside of my elbow and it radiates down my arm.
Doctor: How would you rate your pain on a scale of 1 to 10?
Patient: I would rate it 7 out of 10.
Doctor: I see. Does anything help with the pain, like ibuprofen or ice?
Patient: No, neither ibuprofen nor ice helps.
Doctor: Alright, I understand. Can you tell me about your medical history?
Patient: I have a history of hypertension and diabetes type 2.
Doctor: Okay, and what medications are you taking for these conditions?
Patient: I take lisinopril 10 mg and metformin 500 mg twice a day.
Doctor: Alright. Have you been taking your medications regularly?
Patient: I try to, but sometimes I forget to take my metformin.
Doctor: I see. Can you tell me about your exercise routine?
Patient: I walk 1 to 2 miles per day around my neighborhood and I enjoy working with ceramics and baking cakes and cinnamon rolls for my family.
Doctor: That's great. Now, let's take a look at your vital signs. Your blood pressure is 120/70 mmHg, your pulse rate is appropriate, your respiratory rate is 18 breaths per minute, your oxygen saturation is 100% on room air, and your body temperature is 97.9 degrees F.
Patient: Hmm, okay.
Doctor: And your pain level is 7 out of 10. Have you noticed any changes in your weight?
Patient: Yes, I've gained 5 pounds since my last visit.
Doctor: Alright, let's take a look at your physical exam. I see some tenderness at the medial epicondyle and pain with resisted pronation of your right forearm, flexion, and bending of your right wrist. But there's no bruising or swelling, discoloration, or lesions.
Patient: Yes, that's correct.
Doctor: Alright, I've reviewed the x-ray of your right elbow and it showed no evidence of any fractures or other abnormalities.
Patient: Okay.
Doctor: Based on your examination and radiographic findings, I've diagnosed you with medial epicondylitis. This is likely caused by overuse and damage to the tendons in your arm. I recommend that you wear a sling on your right arm while you're awake to provide increased support, ice your right elbow for 20 minutes 3 times a day, and take ibuprofen 600 mg every 6 hours with food for 1 week. I also advise you to hold off on creating pottery for the next 2 weeks to allow your arm to heal. If you continue to experience persistent elbow pain, we may consider formal physical therapy at your next visit.
Patient: Okay, I understand.
Doctor: I've also encouraged you to check and record your blood sugars every day and increase your metformin to 1000 mg twice a day. I've also recommended that you be referred to diabetic counseling for more information on the steps you can take to manage your diabetes. And I'll order a hemoglobin A1c test for a more accurate assessment of your long-term blood sugar levels.
Patient: Okay, I'll do that.
Doctor: Great. And your hypertension is currently controlled with medication and exercise, so I advise you to continue taking lisinopril 10 mg and continue with your walking routine.
Patient: Okay, I will.
Doctor: Alright, I have all the information I need. You'll need to follow up with me in 2 weeks to review the results of the hemoglobin A1c test and discuss your progress.
Patient: Okay, I will. Thank you.
Doctor: You're welcome. If you have any questions, feel free to ask.
Patient: No, I don't have any questions. Thank you again.
Doctor: You're welcome. Have a good day.
Patient: You too.

