Doctor: Good morning, what brings you in today?

Patient: I have been having back pain.

Doctor: I see. Can you tell me a little more about your history?

Patient: I have a history of diabetes.

Doctor: Okay. And what do you do for a living?

Patient: My family runs a bakery.

Doctor: I see. What medications are you taking?

Patient: I take metformin twice a day.

Doctor: Alright. Let's take a look at your back pain. Can you describe the pain for me?

Patient: It's in my lower back, especially on the left side.

Doctor: Hmm, I see. And have you experienced any numbness or tingling in your feet?

Patient: No, I haven't.

Doctor: Alright. Let's do a physical exam. Can you tell me if you have any redness, discharge, or icterus in your eyes?

Patient: No, I don't have any of those.

Doctor: Okay. And how about your ears?

Patient: The auditory canal and tympanic membranes are clear.

Doctor: Great. And how about your respiratory system? Auscultation of your lungs, are they clear?

Patient: Yes, they are.

Doctor: Okay. And how about your cardiovascular system? Auscultation of your heart, is it regular and rhythmic?

Patient: Yes, it is.

Doctor: Alright. Let's take a look at your back now. Can you tell me if you feel any pain when I press on your upper back?

Patient: No, I don't feel any pain there. But I do feel pain when I press on the midline of my lower back, especially on the left side.

Doctor: Okay. And how about your right lower back?

Patient: There's no pain there.

Doctor: Alright. And can you lift your legs straight up without any pain?

Patient: Yes, I can on the right, but not on the left.

Doctor: Alright. Based on your symptoms and examination, I believe you have a low back strain. I don't expect to see anything significant on your x-ray, but we will obtain one for further evaluation of any possible disc issues.

Patient: Okay.

Doctor: I have referred you to physical therapy to help with the pain and strengthen some of the muscles in your back. And I have prescribed ibuprofen for you to take up to three times a day, and Tylenol for severe pain. I have also prescribed Flexeril for you.

Patient: Okay.

Doctor: And I would like to check your Hemoglobin A1c as you haven't had an updated one in almost 5 months. You will continue with your diet and exercise and continue taking your metformin for the time being. Can you check your blood sugar in the morning and log this information for me?

Patient: Sure, I can do that.

Doctor: Great. And I would like to emphasize the importance of maintaining a healthy lifestyle and avoiding foods high in sugar.

Patient: Okay, I understand.

Doctor: Alright, if you have any questions or concerns, feel free to ask.

Patient: No, I think I understand everything. Thank you, doctor.

Doctor: You're welcome. Take care.

