Doctor: Good morning, Mr. Price. How are you feeling today?

Patient: Good morning, doctor. I'm feeling okay, thank you.

Doctor: I understand you're here for an evaluation of a right knee injury. Can you tell me more about it?

Patient: Yes, I stepped off of a curb and turned over my ankle 2.5 weeks ago and that's when the pain started. It's on the lateral aspect of my right knee.

Doctor: Hmm, I see. And do you have any other symptoms besides the pain?

Patient: Yes, I have clicking with ambulation and locking of my knee with prolonged standing. And I feel pain when I'm resting my foot on my heel.

Doctor: Okay. And do you have any pain at rest, elevated, or when your foot is flat on the ground?

Patient: No, I don't have any pain in those situations.

Doctor: Alright. And you mentioned you're more active during the day, does that impact your knee pain?

Patient: No, it doesn't.

Doctor: Great. Can you perform a full squat without pain?

Patient: Yes, I can.

Doctor: Okay. And you also mentioned numbness and tingling in your right foot. Is that a new symptom?

Patient: No, it's not new. I've had that for a while.

Doctor: I see. And you've been taking ibuprofen and icing your knee 4 times a day, does that provide you with relief?

Patient: Yes, it does. It helps with the pain in the morning and at the end of the day.

Doctor: Alright. Can you tell me about your medical history?

Patient: I've fractured my 2nd toe 3 times over the last 10 years.

Doctor: I see. And what do you do for a living?

Patient: I work in sales.

Doctor: And do you exercise?

Patient: Yes, I ride a bike.

Doctor: Okay. I see that you have a positive history of neurological issues with numbness and tingling in your toes on your right foot from a prior injury.

Patient: Yes, that's correct.

Doctor: Alright. I'm going to examine your right knee now. I see some slight edema and effusion. But there's no pain with palpation of the right lateral knee. I also did a varus test and it was positive. But your ROM is full and Lachman's was negative.

Patient: Hmm, okay.

Doctor: I took 4 views of your right knee and they revealed no evidence of any fractures, dislocations, or bony abnormality. No other abnormalities were noted.

Patient: Okay.

Doctor: Based on your examination and radiographic findings, I have diagnosed you with a right knee lateral collateral ligament strain. I've had a lengthy discussion with you about your symptoms and I've explained that your x-rays did not reveal any acute fractures or bony abnormality. I've recommended that we treat you conservatively with formal physical therapy to strengthen your right knee. I've also prescribed you meloxicam 15 mg to treat your pain. If your pain does not improve with physical therapy, I will recommend obtaining an MRI.

Patient: Okay, I understand.

Doctor: Great. Please make sure to follow up with physical therapy and take your medication as prescribed. If you have any questions or concerns, don't hesitate to ask.

Patient: Thank you, doctor.

