Doctor: Good morning, Christina. How are you feeling today?

Patient: I'm okay, thank you.

Doctor: Can you tell me what brings you to the clinic today?

Patient: I have a non-healing right foot ulcer.

Doctor: I see. Can you tell me more about the history of your present illness?

Patient: It started about a year ago with a blister. I picked at it and it turned red. My primary care doctor prescribed me Keflex, which improved my symptoms, but they didn't go away. I was last on Keflex 5 months ago.

Doctor: Hmm, I understand. Do you have any pain or discomfort in your foot?

Patient: No, I don't have any pain. But I have decreased sensation in my foot.

Doctor: I see. And do you have any increased drainage or fatigue?

Patient: Yes, I have increased drainage and fatigue.

Doctor: Okay. Can you tell me about your medical history?

Patient: I have type 2 diabetes with neuropathy. My blood sugars are running better than they used to be, but they're still in the 200s. My last hemoglobin A1C was 10.

Doctor: I see. And what about your review of systems?

Patient: I have a right foot ulcer and drainage.

Doctor: Okay. Let me take a look at your vital signs. No fever noted. Your vital signs look good today.

Doctor: Now, let me examine your right foot. There is a 1.0 cm x 2.0 cm wound on the dorsal aspect of the lateral right foot, just proximal to the 5th MTP joint. I do see some yellow slough that is present with minimal granulation tissue. No surrounding erythema or cellulitis. There is no evidence of fluid collection. I do not see necrosis. No odor. I do not appreciate any bony exposure. No pain to palpation.

Doctor: The X-ray of your right foot reveals no evidence of osteomyelitis.

Doctor: Based on my examination and the results of your X-ray, I have diagnosed you with a right diabetic foot ulcer. I recommend that we obtain an ankle brachial index to determine your blood supply to see if we can heal the wound. I also recommend that we do a debridement in office today. Are you okay with that?

Patient: Yes, I am.

Doctor: Great. I have also recommended that you be placed in a surgical shoe to provide increased support. I have also prescribed you collagenase ointment to apply to your wound 1 time per day and cover it with a sterile dressing. You should continue this until we see the ulcer decrease in size.

Doctor: You will follow up with me in 2 weeks to check on your progress.

Patient: Okay, thank you.

Doctor: You're welcome. If you have any questions or concerns, don't hesitate to ask.

