dialogue is: {{ Doctor: Hi, how are you feeling today?
Patient: Not so good, doctor. I have been experiencing chest pain on exertion.
Doctor: I see. Can you tell me more about your medical history?
Patient: I have diabetes type 2 and I have not been formally diagnosed with hypertension, but my blood pressures have been trending high.
Doctor: Okay. And what about your family history?
Patient: I have a family history of heart disease.
Doctor: I understand. And what medications are you taking?
Patient: I am taking metformin 500 mg twice daily.
Doctor: Okay. Let's do a review of your systems. Have you been experiencing diaphoresis or clamminess?
Patient: No, I haven't.
Doctor: How about chest pain?
Patient: Yes, I have been experiencing chest pain.
Doctor: And what about dyspnea?
Patient: No, I haven't had any dyspnea.
Doctor: How about nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea?
Patient: No, I haven't had any of those either.
Doctor: Alright. Let's do a physical exam. Can you tell me if you have any swelling or carotid bruits in your neck?
Patient: No, I don't have any swelling or carotid bruits.
Doctor: Alright. Let's check your respiratory system. Can you tell me if you have any wheezes, rales, or rhonchi?
Patient: No, I don't have any of those either.
Doctor: Alright. Let's check your cardiovascular system. Can you tell me if you have any murmurs, gallops, or rubs?
Patient: No, I don't have any of those either.
Doctor: Alright. Let's check your gastrointestinal system. Can you tell me if you have any masses or tenderness in your abdomen?
Patient: No, I don't have any masses or tenderness.
Doctor: Alright. Let's check your musculoskeletal system. Can you tell me if you have any nonpitting edema in your ankles?
Patient: Yes, I have 1+ nonpitting edema in my ankles.
Doctor: Alright. We have done an EKG and it was unremarkable. Your Hemoglobin A1c was 7.0.
Doctor: Based on your history of diabetes, possible history of hypertension, and family history of heart disease, I suspect your chest pain is likely unstable angina. Your recent EKG was unremarkable and there are no other signs of heart attack, but I am worried you may have some sort of a blockage causing your pain.
Doctor: We discussed proper protocol for sublingual nitroglycerin for chest pain. I advised you to go to the emergency department if the medication is not effective after approximately 5 minutes.
Doctor: We will have you start a regimen of baby aspirin at 81 mg daily, for which I have sent in a prescription today. I also sent a prescription for nitroglycerin up to 3 tablets every 5 minutes as needed. Finally, I am going to have you follow up with cardiology for further evaluation.
Doctor: For your type 2 diabetes, you appear to be doing well on your current regimen. I encouraged you to keep your appointment with ophthalmology for evaluation of diabetic retinopathy.
Doctor: I'm going to put in an order for a repeat hemoglobin A1c to check for any needed medication adjustments and have you follow up in 2 weeks. In the meantime, I want you to continue with metformin 500 mg twice daily and dietary modifications.
Doctor: For your hypertension, several providers, including myself, have noted a trend of elevated blood pressures in your chart. I advised you to monitor your blood pressures at home over the next 2 weeks and report your readings to me via MyChart. This should help us determine if any adjustments need to be made to your new medications. We discussed the possible side effects of hydrochlorothiazide how this will hopefully reduce some of your swelling seen on physical exam. All of your questions were answered.
Doctor: You understand and agree with the recommended medical treatment plan.
Patient: Yes, I do.
Doctor: Great. You will follow up in 2 weeks.
Patient: Okay, thank you, doctor.
