Doctor: I see. And since the injury, you've been limping and experiencing a throbbing pain in the medial and lateral aspects of your knee, is that correct?
Patient: Yes, that's right.
Doctor: And how would you rate your pain level on a scale of 1 to 10?
Patient: I would say it's about a 4, but it's increasing.
Doctor: Okay, I understand. Have you tried any pain relief medication?
Patient: Yes, I've been taking Tylenol and it's provided mild relief, but I've also been wrapping my knee with kinesiology tape.
Doctor: I see. And have you been able to take ibuprofen?
Patient: No, I can't take ibuprofen because it would irritate an ulcer I have.
Doctor: Alright, I understand. During your physical exam, we found edema and effusion in your left knee. You also had moderate ROM and pain with flexion and extension of the knee. The Lachman's test was negative, as well as the valgus and varus stress test. Do you have any questions about these findings?
Patient: No, not really.
Doctor: Okay. We took 4 views of your left knee and found no evidence of any fractures or dislocations. No other abnormalities were noted. Based on your examination and radiographic findings, it appears that you have a possible MCL strain.
Patient: Okay.
Doctor: We're going to treat your knee conservatively. We'll put you in a knee immobilizer to provide increased support for the next week and prescribe tramadol 50 mg to treat your pain. You can also take Tylenol as needed for breakthrough pain. I advise you to ice your left knee 3 to 4 times a day for 20 to 30 minutes at a time and refrain from running for the next 3 weeks. If your pain does not improve with the immobilizer, we'll need to obtain an MRI.
Patient: Okay, I understand.
Doctor: Great. If you have any questions or concerns, please don't hesitate to contact me. }}<|eot_id|>
